<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_play_circle — play-button glyph (filled circle + triangle)
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Referenced by (Phase 8 — none yet):
      - future: item_video_preview.xml (overlay on the video
        thumbnail in a video message bubble)
      - future: item_audio_preview.xml (inline play button in
        an audio/voice message bubble)
      - future: item_media_viewer_video.xml (overlay on the
        video page in the full-screen media viewer)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF) with a cutout for the
       triangle. The actual rendered color comes from the
       consumer's tint:
         - Inline audio-bubble button: ?attr/colorPrimary or
           ?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant (theme color).
         - Centered video-overlay button: @android:color/white
           on a semi-transparent black scrim, for legibility
           over any thumbnail.
       Do NOT set a brand color here — the fill is a template.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches every other chrome icon in the app. Renders
       losslessly at any size the consumer requests:
         - 32dp–40dp in an inline audio bubble button.
         - 56dp–64dp in a centered video overlay.
         - 48dp on the media viewer's video page.
       The triangle remains crisp at all these sizes because
       the glyph is designed for legibility at chrome sizes.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL "PLAY_CIRCLE_FILLED" GLYPH.
       This is Google's canonical Material Design
       "play_circle_filled" icon — a solid circle with a
       triangular play head cut out of its interior. It is
       NOT a custom design. Every media player on every
       platform uses essentially this shape, and users
       recognize it instantly as "tap to play."

    4. FILLED CIRCLE, CUT-OUT TRIANGLE (evenOdd).
       The circle is filled (the tint color), and the
       triangle is a transparent region cut out of the
       circle. This is the standard Material construction
       and reads clearly against any background — the
       circle provides the "button" affordance, and the
       cut-out triangle shows the background through,
       making the icon feel integrated with whatever it
       sits on (thumbnail, scrim, player chrome).

       Do NOT switch to "play_circle_outline" (border-only
       circle) or "play_arrow" (triangle only, no circle).
       - play_circle_outline reads as "play button outline"
         on a light background but disappears against
         mid-tone thumbnails.
       - play_arrow (no circle) reads as a small triangular
         arrow, which is used for inline video controls,
         not as the primary "tap to play" affordance.
       The filled-circle-with-cutout-triangle is the
       correct "primary play button" glyph.

    5. SINGLE PATH, evenOdd.
       The path data is a single <path> with the circle and
       the triangle expressed as opposite-winding contours
       (circle CW, triangle CCW in Google's canonical
       asset). fillType="evenOdd" is set explicitly so the
       cutout is robust against any tooling that might
       normalize winding directions in the future. This is
       the same pattern used by ic_check_double, ic_block,
       and ic_nav_profile for their "ring" or "cutout"
       shapes.

    6. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       The play triangle points right. The universal
       convention is NOT to mirror it under RTL — every
       video player, every streaming app, and every
       messaging app keeps the play triangle pointing
       right regardless of text direction. The triangle
       direction is a video-player convention, not a
       reading-direction affordance. Do not add
       auto-mirroring.

    Rendering result at any size:
      A solid circle with a triangular cut-out play head
      — instant recognition as "tap to play."

    ============================================================
    Companion icon (future): ic_pause_circle.xml
    ============================================================
      When the media player is actively playing, the button
      should toggle from play to pause. That requires a
      separate ic_pause_circle.xml (a filled circle with two
      vertical bars cut out of the interior). Do NOT repurpose
      this file or overlay two vertical bars on top of it via
      runtime transforms — create a proper companion icon
      in Phase 8 when the playback toggles are wired. The two
      files will share viewport and general construction, so
      the play/pause state swap is a subtle icon change, not
      a jarring visual shift.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Canonical Material Design "play_circle_filled" path.

        Breakdown:
          Subpath 1 — the outer circle:
            M 12,2               top of the circle
            C 6.48,2 2,6.48 2,12
                                 cubic curve from (12,2) to
                                 (2,12) — the left half of
                                 the circle
            s 4.48,10 10,10      mirror cubic from (2,12) to
                                 (12,22) — the bottom-left
                                 quarter and part of the
                                 bottom half
            s 10,-4.48 10,-10    mirror cubic continuing to
                                 (22,12) — the bottom-right
                                 quarter and start of the
                                 right half
            S 17.52,2 12,2       mirror cubic back to (12,2)
                                 — completing the circle
            z                    close the outer contour

          Subpath 2 — the triangle (play head):
            M 10,16.5            bottom-left corner of the
                                 triangle (tip base)
            v -9                 up 9 units to (10,7.5) —
                                 the triangle's top-left
                                 corner
            l 6,4.5              diagonal down-right to
                                 (16,12) — the triangle's
                                 apex (the "play point")
            l -6,4.5             diagonal down-left back
                                 to (10,16.5) — closing
                                 the triangle
            z                    close the triangle

        Under evenOdd fill, the circle is filled and the
        triangle is a transparent cut-out. The triangle's
        apex points right (toward "play forward" — the
        universal convention), with the flat edge on the
        left (the "back" of the play head).

        Triangle geometry:
          - Base (flat edge) spans y=7.5 to y=16.5 (9 units
            tall) at x=10.
          - Apex is at (16, 12) — 6 units right of the base,
            vertically centered.
          - The triangle occupies roughly 25% of the circle's
            area — large enough to be visible at 24dp, small
            enough to leave a substantial "ring" of the
            circle visible for the button affordance.

        Do NOT resize the triangle to fill more of the circle
        (it would look like a filmstrip "play" button, not a
        general play button), and do NOT shrink it (it would
        become a dot at small sizes). Google's proportions are
        tuned for legibility at every rendering size.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:fillType="evenOdd"
        android:pathData="M12,2C6.48,2 2,6.48 2,12s4.48,10 10,10 10,-4.48 10,-10S17.52,2 12,2zM10,16.5v-9l6,4.5 -6,4.5z" />

</vector>
